{Both Rossolimo and Dr. Tartakower are addicted to playing Bb5 against the Sicilian Defence, wrongly in my opinion [Golombek]. Here indeed it is a positive blunder and much inferior to the normal 4 d4. As a direct consequence Black wins a couple of pawns and breaks up White's K-side. Shortly after the game Rossolimo said to me: 'I sat down with the firm intention of play­ing careful, solid positional chess, and sud­denly found myself two pawns down with a highly critical position. I was occupied in my mind for most of the remainder of the game in inveighing against my foolish carelessness.'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 5. Nxd4 cxd4 6. Ne2 Qg5 7. Bc4 Qxg2 8. Ng3 Qh3 9. c3 Bc5 10. b4 Bd6 11. cxd4 Bxg3 12. Qf3 Bxf2+ 13. Qxf2 Nf6 14. Bf1 Qg4 15. Rg1 Qxe4+ 16. Be2 Nd5 17. d3 Qf5 18. Qxf5 exf5 19. Rxg7 d6 20. a3 Be6 21. Bh6 Kd7 22. Kd2 Rag8 23. Rag1 Rxg7 24. Rxg7 Nf6 25. Bf3 b5 26. Rg5 Rg8 27. Rxg8 Nxg8 28. Bg5 Ne7 {Sources: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek; Manchester Guardian, 22 April 1949} 1/2-1/2

