[SourceQuality "1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bc4 ({This move is so old that it is new. It is probably not as good as} 6. Be2 {but has the advantage of being a great deal less analysed.}) 6... e6 ({Not} 6... g6 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. e5 $1 {with an overwhelming position, for if} dxe5 $4 9. Bxf7+ {(the 'Magnus Smith' variation).}) ({However,} 6... Bd7 {followed by g6 and Bg7 is an excellent line transposing to the Dragon variation in which the WB is misplaced on c4. The text move gives White much more attacking chances.}) 7. Be3 Qc7 8. Qe2 Bd7 9. f4 Rc8 10. Nxc6 ({If} 10. Bb3 {Black intended the following line:} Qa5 11. O-O Be7 12. f5 Nxd4 13. Bxd4 e5 14. Be3 ({Stockfish 18:} 14. Bf2) 14... Rxc3 $1 {(otherwise with Bg5 and Rad1 White will get a considerable positional advantage)} 15. bxc3 Qxc3 {whereupon after} 16. Qd3 Qb4 {Black will win the e-pawn in exchange for his a-pawn and remain with a pawn for the exchange and a strong central pawn mass with chances for both sides.}) 10... bxc6 11. g4 $5 {A double edged move reminding one of the Alekhine-Botvinnik Sicilian at Nottingham, 1936, when Alekhine played g4 before castling, the game ending in an early draw by perpetual check. As in this game Black replied in the only possible way - by an immediate counter attack in the centre.} d5 $1 12. Bd3 ({Not} 12. exd5 cxd5 13. Bxd5 $2 {because of} Bb4 $1 {, winning.}) ({Stockfish 18:} 12. g5 $16) 12... e5 $1 13. exd5 (13. fxe5 $2 Qxe5 {gives Black a winning position.}) 13... Bxg4 14. Qf2 Bd6 ({Not} 14... e4 15. Nxe4 Nxd5 ({Stockfish 18:} 15... Nxe4 16. Bxe4 cxd5 $11) 16. O-O Be7 (16... Nxe3 $2 17. Qxe3 Be7 18. Nf2 $1 {followed by Rad1 with a winning game}) 17. Bd4 O-O 18. f5 $1 {with a winning attack.}) 15. O-O O-O 16. dxc6 Qxc6 17. Nb5 exf4 18. Nxd6 $1 ({Not} 18. Bd4 Bb8 $1 19. Nxa7 (19. Qh4 h5 $1 {and again Black has far the better game}) 19... Bxa7 20. Bxa7 Bh3 21. Rfe1 Ng4 {and Black has a winning game.}) 18... Qxd6 ({If} 18... fxe3 19. Qg3 ({Stockfish 18:} 19. Qh4 $16) 19... Rcd8 20. Rxf6 gxf6 21. Bxh7+ $1 (21. Qxg4+ $2 Kh8 $1 {and Black wins}) 21... Kxh7 22. Qh4+ Kg8 23. Qxg4+ Kh8 {and draws by perpetual check - if} ({Stockfish 18:} 23... Kh7 $1 $11) 24. Qh5+ (24. Qh4+ Kg8 25. Nf5 Qf3 $1) ({Stockfish 18:} 24. Qh3+ $3 Kg8 25. Nf5 $18) 24... Kg8 25. Nf5 Qe4 $1 {and in either case White is forced to take the draw. Having lost to Rossolimo in an earlier round it was necessary for Pachman to play for a win and he therefore rejects this line. As played White gets the advantage of two bishops and will have the better endgame if he can reach it - but meanwhile is faced with a very uncomfortable middle game because of his exposed king.}) 19. Bxf4 Qd5 20. Rfe1 ({If} 20. Qxa7 Qh5 {with a very menacing position. White rightly tries to simplify by exchanging rooks thus reducing the force of the attack. Both sides had now got 10-15 minutes for their last 25 moves!}) 20... Rfe8 21. Rxe8+ Rxe8 22. Re1 Rxe1+ 23. Qxe1 Qxa2 24. Qb4 (24. Qe7 {gives more winning chances. After analysing this position for half an hour or so with both players I [Alexander] have been quite unable to decide who has the better of it so I will leave readers to make their own minds up. Black has two possible lines of play:} Qa1+ {followed by Qa4 or Qa5. My impression is that it should be a draw but that White's chances are slightly better.} ({or} 24... Qe6)) 24... Qe6 25. Qb8+ Qc8 26. Qxa7 Nd5 27. Ba6 $2 ({White now gets his pieces rather out of play. A simple drawing line was} 27. Qb8 Qxb8 28. Bxb8 Nb4 $1 {and a dead drawn 'bishops of the opposite colours' ending follows, (for if} 29. Be4 f5 {and forces the bishop back to d3).}) 27... Qe8 28. Bg3 h5 29. c4 ({Here again} 29. Qb8 Qxb8 30. Bxb8 Nb4 {drawn is best.}) 29... Nb4 30. Bb5 Qe4 31. Qb8+ Kh7 32. Qf4 Qb1+ 33. Qf1 Qxb2 34. Qxf7 Qc1+ $2 ({Black in turn fails to find the best move.} 34... Nd3 $1 {should win e.g.} 35. Qf1 Qd4+ 36. Bf2 Qe4 37. Bg3 ({To stop Nf4: if} 37. Bc6 Qxc4) 37... Be2 38. Qf7 ({or} 38. Bc6 Qe3+ 39. Bf2 Qg5+ 40. Qg2 Qc1+) 38... Bf3 $1 {and wins. The attack by Q, B and N is too strong, the WB being temporarily out of play.}) 35. Qf1 Qe3+ 36. Bf2 Qb3 37. c5 Bh3 38. Qe1 Qf3 39. Bf1 Nd3 40. Qe2 Qxe2 41. Bxe2 {Sources: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek; The Times, 22 April 1949} (41. Bxe2 {After} Nxf2 42. Kxf2 {the passed c-pawn will cost Black his bishop, leaving White with bishop and wrong coloured rook pawn. An extraordinarily complicated and interesting struggle, fought with the greatest determination by both players.}) 1/2-1/2

