1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Bc5 4. c3 Qf6 5. d3 ({Black's choice of opening was based on a suggestion in the tournament book of Mun­ich, 1941. If White had played} 5. d4 exd4 6. e5 Qg6 7. cxd4 Bb4+ 8. Nc3 d5 9. O-O Nge7 10. Qb3 {(Recommended by M.C.O.) then Black intended to continue with Haberditz's suggestion:} O-O $1 {e.g.,} 11. Bxc6 Nxc6 12. Qxd5 Be6 13. Qb5 Bh3 14. Ne1 Nxd4 $1 {and wins.}) 5... h6 6. Nbd2 Nge7 7. Nf1 ({The line adopted by Thomas was also played by Kieninger v. Alekhine at Munich, but does not lead to more than a minimal advantage for White. White’s 7 Nf1 is rather better than Kieninger's} 7. Nc4) 7... O-O 8. Be3 Bb6 9. Bxb6 axb6 10. Ne3 d6 11. O-O Ra7 ({Weak: he should simply play} 11... Ng6 12. d4 Nf4 {with equality. After this White has a good game.}) 12. d4 Ng6 13. Re1 Bd7 14. a3 Re8 15. d5 Nb8 16. Bxd7 Nxd7 17. g3 Nc5 18. Nf5 ({Better is} 18. Ng2 {followed by c4 and a general advance on the Q-side.}) 18... Ne7 ({Black must not play} 18... Nxe4 {at once, as after} 19. Nxh6+ {he cannot take with the queen and his king's position is weak.}) 19. N3h4 Nxf5 20. Nxf5 Nxe4 {The position is perhaps a little in Black's favour, but certainly not enough to justify taking risks to win.} 21. Nxh6+ Qxh6 22. Rxe4 f5 23. Rc4 Re7 24. Qf3 Qg6 25. Re1 Ra5 26. Rd1 Rc5 {Source: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek} 1/2-1/2

