1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 cxd4 5. cxd4 Nc6 6. Nf3 Nge7 7. Be2 Nf5 8. b3 Bb4+ 9. Kf1 O-O 10. g4 ({10 g4 is a serious weakening of the H-side that eventually leads to disaster. Better is} 10. a3 Ba5 11. b4 Bb6 12. Bb2 {followed by g3 and Kg2.}) 10... Nfe7 11. a3 Ba5 12. Bb2 ({White plays too tamely with the defenive Bb2: better is} 12. Bg5 f6 13. exf6 gxf6 14. Bh6 {with counterplay.}) 12... f6 13. Bd3 fxe5 14. dxe5 Bb6 15. Nbd2 Bd7 16. Qb1 Ng6 17. Bxg6 hxg6 18. Qxg6 Ne7 19. Qg5 Rc8 20. Kg2 Qe8 21. Bd4 Ng6 22. Bxb6 Nf4+ 23. Kg3 axb6 24. h4 Rc3 25. Kh2 d4 26. Ne4 Rxf3 27. Nf6+ Rxf6 28. exf6 Qg6 29. Qxg6 Nxg6 30. h5 Nf4 31. fxg7 Bc6 32. Kg1 Ne2+ 33. Kh2 Rxf2+ 34. Kh3 Bxh1 35. Rxh1 Kxg7 36. g5 d3 {Source: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek} 0-1

