1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Be7 4. d4 d6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. Be3 O-O 7. h3 Nxe4 8. Nxe4 d5 9. Nxe5 $2 ({A terrifying little example of the power of two bishops and the evils of neglected development. Instead of 9 Nxe5? White should play} 9. Bd3 {with quite a good game. After this the tripled pawns are a positive help to Black since they allow his bishops such line open lines for action.}) 9... dxc4 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Nc5 ({Weak though he is already in a bad way. The best chance is} 11. Qd2 {followed by 0-0-0.}) 11... f5 12. Qf3 $2 f4 13. Bxf4 Qxd4 14. Be3 Qxb2 15. Qd1 Qb4+ 16. c3 Qxc3+ 17. Kf1 Qxe3 {Sources: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek; Manchester Guardian, 25 April 1949; BCM, June 1949, p194} 0-1

