{The longest game of the tournament and, not only a monument to the industry and pertinacity of the first prize winner, but also a tribute to his capacity for elegant com­binative ideas (see the last ten moves). This game was adjourned three times and consequently was the last to finish in the tournament. It was very fitting that Rossolimo should win first prize by the neat final queen sacrifice.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 Nbd7 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 a5 8. Qc2 Nc5 9. Be3 Ng4 10. Bxc5 dxc5 11. h3 Nh6 12. g4 f6 13. O-O-O Nf7 14. Rdg1 Nd6 15. h4 Bd7 16. h5 g5 17. Nd2 h6 18. Nf1 O-O 19. Ne3 Kh8 20. Bd3 Qb8 21. Kd2 Rd8 22. Ke2 Bf8 23. Rb1 Re8 24. f3 Be7 25. Rhc1 Rf8 26. Qd1 Qe8 27. Bc2 Qf7 28. Ba4 Bxa4 29. Qxa4 Qe8 30. Qc2 Qd7 31. Kf2 Rfb8 32. b3 Bf8 33. Na4 b6 34. Nc3 Be7 35. Qe2 Bf8 36. Nc2 Ra7 37. Na3 Raa8 38. Nab5 Nxb5 39. cxb5 Bd6 40. Qc4 Ra7 41. Rc2 Rba8 42. Qa4 Kh7 43. Ne2 Kh8 44. Ng3 Kh7 45. Nf5 Qd8 46. Kg3 Qd7 47. Rbc1 Qd8 48. Kh3 Qd7 49. Ne3 Kh8 50. Nf5 Kh7 51. Rc4 Qd8 52. Kg2 Qd7 53. Kf1 Qd8 54. Ke2 Qd7 55. Ke1 Qd8 56. Kf2 Qd7 57. Kg1 Qd8 58. Kh1 Qd7 59. Kh2 Qd8 60. Kg3 Qd7 61. Rb1 Qd8 62. a3 Qd7 63. Rcc1 Qd8 64. Qc4 Qd7 65. Qe2 Qd8 66. Rc2 a4 $2 67. b4 cxb4 68. Nxd6 b3 69. Rc6 ({Stockfish 18:} 69. Nf5 $5 bxc2 70. Qxc2 $16) 69... cxd6 70. Qc4 Kg7 71. Rc1 Kf7 72. Qb4 Ke7 73. R1c4 Qb8 74. Qc3 Kd7 75. Kf2 Rb7 76. Ke2 Rba7 77. Kd2 Rb7 78. Kc1 Rba7 79. Kb1 Rb7 80. f4 $1 $18 Rba7 81. Qh3 Qd8 82. fxg5 fxg5 83. Rc1 $6 ({Stockfish 18:} 83. Rc3 $1 Ke8 84. Qf1 Rf7 85. Qc1 $1 $18) 83... Qe7 $2 ({Stockfish 18:} 83... Ke8 $1 84. Qe3 $1 (84. Rf1 $6 Rf7 85. Rf5 Kf8 $1 86. Qe3 $5 Raa7 $13) 84... Rb8 85. Rf1 Rf7 86. Rf5 {and maybe White's winning?}) 84. Qc3 Qd8 85. Rf1 Ke7 86. Rf5 Rd7 87. Qxe5+ {Source: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek} 1-0

