1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. dxe5 Nxb5 7. a4 Nbd4 8. Nxd4 Nxd4 9. Qxd4 d5 {The variation up to Black's 9th move is a pet line of Fawcett's. These 9 moves were given to Pachman as a pre-requisite of play. When I showed him the line the previous day he viewed it with much disgust, claim­ing it gave the better game to Black and that the best White could hope for was a draw — a view with which I concurred. But after some overnight study he came to me bubbling over with enthusiasm and said he had found the line gave White excellent chances.} 10. Nc3 c6 11. Be3 Bf5 12. f4 Be7 13. Rf2 O-O 14. g4 Be6 15. f5 c5 16. Qf4 g5 17. Qf3 Qd7 18. f6 Bxg4 19. Qg3 d4 20. fxe7 Rfe8 21. Bxg5 dxc3 22. Qf4 Bh5 23. Bf6 cxb2 24. Rb1 Bg6 {"White announces mate in 5 moves" Source: 'Southsea Chess Tournament 1949' by Harry Golombek. n.b. the consultation partners' full names: Edward Douglas Fawcett, Wilfred Henry Pratten, Borland (either John Alexander Charles Borland or Robert Edmond Borland who were brothers)} 1-0

